<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client Go</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-go</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Thin Maven wrapper over the Go toolchain (go.mod beside this file). The commands below run
        ONLY under -Dpc.foreignClients - see the foreign-clients profile in the clients aggregator
        for why opt-in is load-bearing - and both go through bin/foreign-client-step.sh, which
        decides what an ABSENT go means: a banner and a skip by default, red under
        -Dpc.foreign.strict=1, which is what the CI row passes. The commands themselves are declared
        here and nowhere else (astubbs#242).

        THE TESTS NEED THE SIDECARS, and the go-e2e-harness profile below is what gets them. Two Go
        tests spawn a sidecar over the real wire - the end-to-end one drives TestModeMain from
        parallel-consumer-proxy's test jar, the handshake one drives its production Main - and with
        no Maven dependency at all `-am` did not pull the proxy into the reactor, so the CI row's own
        command, `./mvnw test -pl :parallel-consumer-proxy-client-go -am -Dpc.foreignClients`, ran
        the Go test against an unbuilt `parallel-consumer-proxy/target` and failed there. The profile
        declares that dependency in the opt-in lane only, exactly as the Python and TypeScript
        modules do, and the aggregator's hoisted execution writes the resolved classpath where the
        tests read it.

        The Go tests still fail rather than skipping when the sidecar is absent, because a test that
        quietly does not run is not a passing test. Recorded as a wave finding in
        docs/inflight/clients/go.md.

        TWO FLAGS ON THE TEST COMMAND, AND NEITHER IS DECORATION.

        -count=1 DEFEATS GO'S TEST CACHE, and it is here because the cache produced a false pass that
        was caught by accident. `go test` caches a package's result keyed on its own inputs, and the
        sidecar this module spawns is not one of them - so breaking the sidecar's refusal on purpose,
        to prove the handshake test could fail, returned `ok (cached)` and the arm read as green.
        A CI row is a fresh checkout and never sees it; a developer changing the engine and re-running
        the Go client does, silently, which is the worse half.

        -race is the Go client's own concurrency check, and it is the cheapest real win available on
        the whole polyglot surface: this is the client with the executor fan-out, the detector is
        built into the toolchain, and it costs one flag.
        docs/inflight/static-polyglot-client-analysers.md ranked it first of five and recorded that
        the gating recipe did not use it while a per-language note claimed it did.
    -->
    <properties>
        <pc.foreign.skip>false</pc.foreign.skip>
        <pc.foreign.build.executable>bash</pc.foreign.build.executable>
        <pc.foreign.build.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool go -- go build ./...</pc.foreign.build.args>
        <pc.foreign.test.executable>bash</pc.foreign.test.executable>
        <pc.foreign.test.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool go -- go test -race -count=1 ./...</pc.foreign.test.args>
    </properties>

    <profiles>
        <!--
            THE SIDECARS THE SUITE DRIVES, AND WHY THEY ARE A DEPENDENCY.

            The Go suite's end-to-end test spawns the real test-mode sidecar over the real wire, and
            its handshake test spawns the production Main - which between them need the proxy
            module's test jar (TestModeMain and the harness), its main jar, and core's test jar
            underneath. Declaring them here is what makes the CI matrix row's exact command build
            those modules first and resolve them; the aggregator's hoisted `sidecar-classpath`
            execution writes the resulting classpath to target/sidecar-classpath.txt, where
            internal/harness reads it.

            SCOPED TO THIS PROFILE DELIBERATELY. No client depends on the engine, and with the
            profile inactive - every ordinary build - there is no dependency edge here at all, so
            an ordinary scoped build still runs no Go toolchain and never reaches the engine. The
            edge exists only in the opt-in lane that runs the foreign test suite. The Go client
            itself obtains the protocol the way every non-JVM client does, by generating from the
            .proto by path, with no Maven dependency. Modelled on the python-e2e-harness profile.

            NOT NEEDED BY THE CONFORMANCE RUNNER. cmd/conformance-runner is driven by the shared
            suite (parallel-consumer-proxy-conformance), which hosts the engine in its OWN JVM and
            hands the runner a sidecar to spawn - so that lane needs nothing from here.
        -->
        <profile>
            <id>go-e2e-harness</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>pc.foreignClients</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <type>test-jar</type>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
                <dependency>
                    <!-- LongPollingMockConsumer and the harness's logback-test.xml -->
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-core</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <type>test-jar</type>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
            <build>
                <plugins>
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                        <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                        <!-- Declaring it is the opt-in; the aggregator's pluginManagement supplies
                             the version and the `sidecar-classpath` execution. -->
                    </plugin>
                </plugins>
            </build>
        </profile>
    </profiles>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <!--
                NO maven-clean-plugin FILESET HERE, AND THAT IS A MEASURED RESULT RATHER THAN AN
                OMISSION - the note exists because "nothing to clean" and "nobody wired the clean"
                look identical in a pom (astubbs#242).

                GO WRITES NOTHING INTO THIS DIRECTORY. `go build ./...` matches several packages, and
                for that form the toolchain compiles as a check and DISCARDS the objects - verified
                by running it in a pristine tree and finding not one new file, tracked or ignored.
                What it produces instead goes to the build cache, GOCACHE (~/.cache/go-build), with
                the modules in GOMODCACHE (~/go/pkg/mod). Both are outside the module, both are
                shared with every other checkout and agent on the machine, and both are this
                language's ~/.m2 - so `go clean -cache` or `-modcache` is exactly the deletion this
                repo forbids, not the one it wants. `go vet` and `go tool staticcheck` are the same
                story.

                THE BUILT ARTEFACTS ARE ALREADY COVERED, because every one of them lands in target/.
                The shared conformance suite prebuilds
                `go build -o target/conformance-runner ./cmd/conformance-runner` - grep
                `target/conformance-runner` in the conformance module's LanguageRunners - and the
                demo's run.sh writes `target/pc-go-demo` and `target/demo-sidecar-classpath.txt`.
                Maven's default clean takes target/ whole, no configuration needed. Proven both ways:
                the conformance binary is there after the suite's prebuild and gone after
                `mvn clean`.

                THE DEMO IS A NESTED GO MODULE (demo/go.mod), so `go build ./...` above does not
                descend into it and this module's Maven build never compiles it - demo/run.sh and
                demo/Dockerfile do. demo/README.md says why the module is nested: franz-go must not
                reach an application that uses the client library.

                gen/ IS SOURCE, NOT OUTPUT. The protobuf stubs under gen/ are committed on purpose,
                because Go has no codegen step at `go get` time - scripts/generate-proto.sh says so
                in its header. Deleting them would break a consumer of this module, and the
                regeneration check is `git status` staying clean, not a clean removing them.

                So if a future change here starts emitting into the module - a `go build -o` landing
                outside target/, a `go test -c` binary - it needs a fileset added below, and this
                paragraph is the reason nobody had to write one before.
            -->
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
